1
00:00:02,830 --> 00:00:05,250
Hello everyone and welcome to EnglishPod.

2
00:00:05,530 --> 00:00:05,930
My name is Marco.

3
00:00:06,350 --> 00:00:10,170
My name is Catherine and today we have a very serious dialogue for you.

4
00:00:10,430 --> 00:00:10,810
That's right.

5
00:00:11,010 --> 00:00:15,770
We are going to talk about a will and testament, the last will and testament of someone.

6
00:00:16,170 --> 00:00:17,850
Alright, so what is a will and testament?

7
00:00:18,200 --> 00:00:29,360
Well basically this is a document that someone writes, usually with a lawyer, that explains what to do with the person's body and their possessions, their things, after they die.

8
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:38,800
So many people write these and the family will then use it to discover who gets to receive certain objects and what to do with this person's body.

9
00:00:38,800 --> 00:00:39,690
Right, exactly.

10
00:00:40,110 --> 00:00:42,050
So that is a last will and testament.

11
00:00:42,530 --> 00:00:49,880
We are going to take a look at an example of one so you would see more or less in English how one is written.

12
00:00:50,160 --> 00:00:53,280
Now obviously they can be very long and very complex.

13
00:00:53,280 --> 00:01:00,520
So we've taken one that has some interesting words there that are a little bit more specific, a lot of legal terms there.

14
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:04,620
So why don't we listen to this dialogue for the first time and we'll be back shortly.

15
00:01:11,800 --> 00:01:13,680
Last will and testament of Luke Thompson.

16
00:01:14,880 --> 00:01:22,600
I Luke Thompson residing in California, being of sound mind, do hereby declare this instrument to be my last will and testament.

17
00:01:23,420 --> 00:01:26,530
I hereby revoke all previous wills and codicils.

18
00:01:27,230 --> 00:01:30,290
I direct that the disposition of my remains be as follows.

19
00:01:31,360 --> 00:01:37,700
I'm to be cremated and taken to the summit of Mount Everest where my ashes will forever remain at the ceiling of the earth.

20
00:01:38,780 --> 00:01:45,840
I give all the rest and residue of my estate to my spouse, Betty Thompson, should she survive me for 60 days.

21
00:01:47,080 --> 00:01:54,760
If my spouse, Betty Thompson, does not survive me, I give all the rest and residue of my estate to English Pod.

22
00:01:54,760 --> 00:02:05,790
If neither Betty Thompson nor English Pod survives me, I give all the rest and residue of my estate to my heirs as determined by the laws of the state of California relating to descent and distribution.

23
00:02:06,920 --> 00:02:11,880
I appoint Robert Porter to act as the executor of this will, to serve without bond.

24
00:02:12,840 --> 00:02:19,460
Should Robert Porter be unable or unwilling to serve, then I appoint Jason Smalls to act as the executor of this will.

25
00:02:20,460 --> 00:02:32,600
I herewith affix my signature to this will on this the 23rd of May, 2010 in the presence of the following witnesses who witnessed and subscribed this will at my request and in my presence.

26
00:02:33,080 --> 00:02:36,080
All right, we're back.

27
00:02:38,640 --> 00:02:42,020
So now we're going to take a look at five key words on Language Takeaway.

28
00:02:45,080 --> 00:02:45,610
Language Takeaway.

29
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:52,810
Okay, the first word here in Language Takeaway comes from the very beginning of this monologue.

30
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:55,360
We're reading the last will in Testament aloud.

31
00:02:55,770 --> 00:03:00,630
I Luke Thompson residing in California being of sound mind.

32
00:03:01,090 --> 00:03:02,370
Sound as an adjective.

33
00:03:02,710 --> 00:03:03,110
What does this mean?

34
00:03:03,450 --> 00:03:04,250
That's very interesting.

35
00:03:04,630 --> 00:03:12,250
So he says, I am in sound mind and sound is an adjective to describe I am in a good state of mind.

36
00:03:12,790 --> 00:03:13,130
That's right.

37
00:03:13,780 --> 00:03:19,720
So it means not having any problems or logical or unaffected.

38
00:03:19,720 --> 00:03:20,330
Basically I'm not crazy.

39
00:03:20,790 --> 00:03:21,090
Right.

40
00:03:21,510 --> 00:03:22,110
I'm not crazy.

41
00:03:22,750 --> 00:03:23,690
I don't have any problems.

42
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:25,840
We can say this about other things too.

43
00:03:25,840 --> 00:03:27,600
We can say he's of sound body.

44
00:03:27,930 --> 00:03:28,070
Right.

45
00:03:28,290 --> 00:03:29,390
That means that he is healthy.

46
00:03:29,830 --> 00:03:31,390
He has no problems with his body.

47
00:03:31,970 --> 00:03:34,450
Or I could say, Marco, that's a really good decision.

48
00:03:34,770 --> 00:03:35,930
It's a sound decision.

49
00:03:36,410 --> 00:03:40,770
So again, that's also a way of saying good or it will work.

50
00:03:40,970 --> 00:03:41,430
It will work.

51
00:03:41,670 --> 00:03:42,090
It's a good decision.

52
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:44,040
Right.

53
00:03:44,040 --> 00:03:50,320
So you can use this adjective with sound body, sound mind or a sound decision.

54
00:03:50,320 --> 00:03:56,040
Or sometimes some people even say, I think in Ireland, they say, oh, he's a really sound guy.

55
00:03:56,040 --> 00:03:57,040
Oh, so he's a good guy.

56
00:03:57,040 --> 00:03:58,040
He's a good guy.

57
00:03:58,040 --> 00:03:59,040
Very interesting.

58
00:03:59,040 --> 00:04:00,040
Yeah.

59
00:04:00,040 --> 00:04:02,800
So sound body, sound mind, sound guy.

60
00:04:02,800 --> 00:04:09,520
Moving on, he was talking about the will and he says, I hereby revoke all previous wills and codicils.

61
00:04:09,520 --> 00:04:12,120
The verb here is to revoke.

62
00:04:12,120 --> 00:04:13,120
To revoke.

63
00:04:13,120 --> 00:04:19,000
This is something that a lot of people hear in America when they drive their car too fast.

64
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:22,900
The police catch them and the police revoke their driver's license.

65
00:04:23,140 --> 00:04:25,240
And that basically means to take away.

66
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:25,980
That's right.

67
00:04:26,480 --> 00:04:27,840
Or to take, right?

68
00:04:27,840 --> 00:04:28,560
To take away from.

69
00:04:28,820 --> 00:04:34,360
So this man here, Luke Thompson, what he's saying is, I revoke all previous wills.

70
00:04:34,620 --> 00:04:38,940
That means any other will that I wrote before, it's not good anymore.

71
00:04:39,500 --> 00:04:40,340
I'm throwing it away.

72
00:04:40,680 --> 00:04:42,280
This is the only one that will exist.

73
00:04:43,170 --> 00:04:45,870
So you can use this word revoke with many aspects.

74
00:04:46,170 --> 00:04:51,810
For example, sometimes employees will get their benefits revoked by the company because maybe they don't have enough money anymore.

75
00:04:52,190 --> 00:04:53,570
Or maybe they're bad employees.

76
00:04:53,730 --> 00:04:54,690
Or maybe they were bad employees.

77
00:04:54,800 --> 00:04:55,800
That's too bad.

78
00:04:55,850 --> 00:04:56,130
All right.

79
00:04:56,800 --> 00:04:56,950
So to revoke.

80
00:04:57,800 --> 00:05:00,530
Now, moving on, he was talking about his estate.

81
00:05:01,520 --> 00:05:07,370
So this is very interesting because he says, I give all the rest and residue of my estate to my spouse.

82
00:05:08,070 --> 00:05:10,670
Okay, estate, it sounds like a very fancy word.

83
00:05:10,850 --> 00:05:11,810
It is a fancy word.

84
00:05:12,190 --> 00:05:15,510
Sometimes we use this word to mean someone's property, their house.

85
00:05:16,250 --> 00:05:19,750
But in a will, this means everything the person owns.

86
00:05:20,490 --> 00:05:25,310
House, car, things, pets, stocks, investments.

87
00:05:26,050 --> 00:05:31,590
The estate is essentially everything the person owns as a kind of one unit.

88
00:05:31,810 --> 00:05:32,690
Right, exactly.

89
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:36,280
So anything that is yours or you may have bought.

90
00:05:36,280 --> 00:05:38,610
Just consider the estate is a big bag, right?

91
00:05:38,960 --> 00:05:39,430
That's your estate.

92
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:41,950
And inside this bag is everything that you own.

93
00:05:42,480 --> 00:05:46,850
You might see advertisements in fact in America or in England for estate sales.

94
00:05:47,760 --> 00:05:54,070
This means that when someone passes away, when they die, their family will sell their things and this is an estate sale.

95
00:05:54,360 --> 00:05:57,360
You can find beautiful, interesting things in estate sales.

96
00:05:57,360 --> 00:05:58,360
Exactly.

97
00:05:58,360 --> 00:05:59,360
Very interesting.

98
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:00,860
So that is a person's estate.

99
00:06:00,860 --> 00:06:05,010
Now remember this is in the legal terms because you also have other things like real estate.

100
00:06:05,760 --> 00:06:08,480
And as you mentioned, which would be a house, right?

101
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:17,360
Okay, now apart from his estate, he was telling everyone as to how they should take care of his body or what they should do with it.

102
00:06:17,360 --> 00:06:19,510
And he said, well, I am to be cremated.

103
00:06:20,010 --> 00:06:20,790
Okay, this is important.

104
00:06:21,390 --> 00:06:24,210
When a person dies, there are generally two options.

105
00:06:25,110 --> 00:06:29,550
Their body can be buried in a casket in the ground.

106
00:06:30,570 --> 00:06:31,850
That means they're put in the earth.

107
00:06:32,050 --> 00:06:34,590
But a lot of people choose to be cremated.

108
00:06:34,800 --> 00:06:44,680
Okay, that means that their body will be burned and their ashes, that means the things that are left after they are burned, they will be taken somewhere else.

109
00:06:44,680 --> 00:06:45,910
Like in a bowl or a pot.

110
00:06:47,280 --> 00:06:49,110
And in this case, that's what the person wants.

111
00:06:49,360 --> 00:06:50,850
He wants to be cremated.

112
00:06:51,200 --> 00:06:57,040
So that would be the verb to cremate and the noun would be a cremation.

113
00:06:57,040 --> 00:06:58,810
And what is going to happen to all this stuff?

114
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:06,280
Basically it's going to be given away to his wife, to English Pod, and to his heirs.

115
00:07:06,950 --> 00:07:07,850
This is our next word.

116
00:07:08,080 --> 00:07:09,080
What is an heir?

117
00:07:09,150 --> 00:07:12,090
Now, if you notice, the H is silent there.

118
00:07:12,290 --> 00:07:13,330
So his heirs.

119
00:07:14,190 --> 00:07:16,070
And those are basically his children.

120
00:07:17,040 --> 00:07:19,280
His daughters or his sons.

121
00:07:20,140 --> 00:07:27,320
Right, my heirs are the people who are related to me, who in this case will receive some of my things.

122
00:07:27,820 --> 00:07:33,600
Now, is it only the children or also it could be maybe any other relative?

123
00:07:33,600 --> 00:07:34,760
It could be any other relative.

124
00:07:34,760 --> 00:07:38,040
You could say, I'm his heir, he's my great uncle.

125
00:07:38,040 --> 00:07:44,240
It means we're related but we're not necessarily father and daughter or grandfather and granddaughter.

126
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:46,440
An heir is someone who is related to the family.

127
00:07:47,000 --> 00:07:48,680
It doesn't have to be a certain relationship.

128
00:07:49,160 --> 00:07:51,920
And now it's interesting because from this word we can get inheritance.

129
00:07:52,520 --> 00:07:54,000
Now we didn't find it in the dialogue.

130
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:58,740
So it's very interesting because they're very similar in spelling, right?

131
00:07:59,640 --> 00:08:00,060
Inheritance and heir.

132
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:01,500
Right, good to remember.

133
00:08:01,940 --> 00:08:02,800
Yeah, good to remember.

134
00:08:03,360 --> 00:08:04,840
You switch the E and the I's, right?

135
00:08:04,900 --> 00:08:05,120
That's right.

136
00:08:05,840 --> 00:08:08,040
So inheritance is a thing.

137
00:08:08,040 --> 00:08:09,060
You can receive this.

138
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:12,960
So I received an inheritance from my great grandmother.

139
00:08:13,680 --> 00:08:16,140
That means I received some money or some things.

140
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:18,480
That means you were that person's heir.

141
00:08:18,920 --> 00:08:19,920
That's right.

142
00:08:19,920 --> 00:08:20,920
Okay.

143
00:08:20,920 --> 00:08:24,480
Or in his inheritance, he received a large house.

144
00:08:24,480 --> 00:08:26,240
He was the heir to a great fortune.

145
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:27,500
Okay, interesting.

146
00:08:28,400 --> 00:08:32,880
So again, inheritance wasn't in the dialogue but I think it's worth mentioning.

147
00:08:32,880 --> 00:08:39,860
And I think now we can move on to four very interesting items that we have for you on Fluency Builder.

148
00:08:40,440 --> 00:08:45,760
Okay, these first two are words that we normally read or hear in legal documents.

149
00:08:45,760 --> 00:08:50,860
And I want to place emphasis on the fact that we don't really say them in spoken English.

150
00:08:51,400 --> 00:08:54,640
So remember that these are more formal terms.

151
00:08:55,340 --> 00:08:57,180
The first one is hereby.

152
00:08:58,380 --> 00:08:59,400
I hereby declare.

153
00:09:00,640 --> 00:09:02,020
That means now.

154
00:09:03,220 --> 00:09:08,340
With this word, with this spoken sentence, this will happen.

155
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:10,720
I think we've seen it like in movies sometimes.

156
00:09:11,100 --> 00:09:14,340
I hereby declare this country in the name of England.

157
00:09:14,820 --> 00:09:16,740
I hereby declare you man and wife.

158
00:09:17,160 --> 00:09:17,460
Exactly.

159
00:09:18,320 --> 00:09:19,320
Okay.

160
00:09:19,320 --> 00:09:20,600
That means in this moment, this happens.

161
00:09:21,280 --> 00:09:21,540
Okay.

162
00:09:22,280 --> 00:09:26,600
And a very similar word that we also saw in the dialogue is herewith.

163
00:09:26,600 --> 00:09:31,970
So in the Will and Testament, we hear Luke Thompson who says, I herewith affix my signature.

164
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:39,330
That means with the action of signing my name, signing my signature, this will be completed.

165
00:09:40,610 --> 00:09:43,570
So I herewith, you have to have an action, not just a word.

166
00:09:43,830 --> 00:09:44,070
Okay.

167
00:09:44,880 --> 00:09:46,600
So I herewith.

168
00:09:46,600 --> 00:09:48,730
I herewith attach my seal.

169
00:09:49,980 --> 00:09:51,080
A seal is like a stamp.

170
00:09:51,760 --> 00:09:54,400
Very common in the old days when you had real letters.

171
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:00,600
But I herewith attach my seal and give my approval to this document.

172
00:10:01,160 --> 00:10:01,440
Okay.

173
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:02,960
So it has to be something physical.

174
00:10:03,200 --> 00:10:04,200
Yeah.

175
00:10:04,200 --> 00:10:05,200
Right.

176
00:10:05,200 --> 00:10:10,360
So if maybe you're writing like this very classic letter and you say, I herewith include a lock of my hair.

177
00:10:10,560 --> 00:10:11,220
Oh, how romantic.

178
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:11,720
Okay.

179
00:10:11,840 --> 00:10:12,460
That's kind of creepy.

180
00:10:13,360 --> 00:10:17,880
But you're including or attaching physically, right?

181
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:21,160
I guess the modern version of herewith would be attach.

182
00:10:21,160 --> 00:10:22,160
Yeah.

183
00:10:22,160 --> 00:10:23,160
Okay.

184
00:10:23,160 --> 00:10:24,160
Good.

185
00:10:24,160 --> 00:10:25,160
Herewith, hereby.

186
00:10:25,160 --> 00:10:28,320
We've got another phrase here that you might hear more commonly.

187
00:10:29,160 --> 00:10:32,680
The beginning of the Will and Testament, there's a phrase as follows.

188
00:10:33,360 --> 00:10:33,700
Okay.

189
00:10:34,520 --> 00:10:38,100
I direct that the disposition of my remains be as follows.

190
00:10:38,400 --> 00:10:40,360
So with my body do as follows.

191
00:10:41,160 --> 00:10:51,100
So basically this is a very common way of putting this in a sentence to indicate that you're going to start to list items.

192
00:10:51,640 --> 00:10:51,980
That's right.

193
00:10:52,140 --> 00:10:52,720
Or give instructions.

194
00:10:53,840 --> 00:10:54,840
Give instructions.

195
00:10:54,840 --> 00:10:57,760
So for example, to make a loaf of bread, do as follows.

196
00:10:57,760 --> 00:11:00,080
Number one, get some flour.

197
00:11:00,080 --> 00:11:01,140
Number two, add some water.

198
00:11:01,640 --> 00:11:06,260
So this is a way for us to say, listen, I'm going to give you instructions or a list.

199
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:06,900
Pay attention.

200
00:11:07,640 --> 00:11:09,260
So please do as follows.

201
00:11:10,020 --> 00:11:11,360
Please act as follows.

202
00:11:11,840 --> 00:11:12,840
Okay.

203
00:11:12,840 --> 00:11:13,840
Very good.

204
00:11:13,840 --> 00:11:21,040
And our last word that we have here, going back to herewith, he said, I herewith affix my signature.

205
00:11:21,040 --> 00:11:22,310
What does it mean when you affix?

206
00:11:22,860 --> 00:11:23,380
Okay.

207
00:11:23,520 --> 00:11:29,840
Affix is a word we have a number of other words for these days, so you rarely hear it.

208
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:31,800
It means to attach or to include.

209
00:11:32,280 --> 00:11:33,280
Okay.

210
00:11:33,280 --> 00:11:36,200
So I herewith affix my signature would be attach my signature.

211
00:11:36,520 --> 00:11:36,660
Right.

212
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:43,920
I herewith provide my signature or I herewith affix my address or I herewith affix something.

213
00:11:44,100 --> 00:11:44,380
Okay.

214
00:11:44,780 --> 00:11:45,180
Very good.

215
00:11:45,620 --> 00:11:46,780
Really interesting stuff.

216
00:11:47,280 --> 00:11:56,300
You'll obviously see this very commonly with legal documents such as these, even though it is like older English, it's still very commonly used.

217
00:11:56,360 --> 00:11:58,320
I guess because the law is so old.

218
00:11:58,320 --> 00:11:58,480
That's right.

219
00:11:58,580 --> 00:11:58,880
Definitely.

220
00:12:00,320 --> 00:12:03,960
And I think that it's worth listening to today's monologue one more time.

221
00:12:03,960 --> 00:12:15,560
Think about some of the words that we've just discussed and we'll be back in a moment.

222
00:12:15,560 --> 00:12:17,420
Last will and testament of Luke Thompson.

223
00:12:18,620 --> 00:12:26,360
I Luke Thompson residing in California, being of sound mind do hereby declare this instrument to be my last will and testament.

224
00:12:27,160 --> 00:12:30,250
I hereby revoke all previous wills and codicils.

225
00:12:31,030 --> 00:12:34,030
I direct that the disposition of my remains be as follows.

226
00:12:35,110 --> 00:12:41,450
I'm to be cremated and taken to the summit of Mount Everest where my ashes will forever remain at the ceiling of the earth.

227
00:12:42,220 --> 00:12:49,560
I give all the rest and residue of my estate to my spouse, Betty Thompson, should she survive me for 60 days.

228
00:12:50,800 --> 00:12:58,480
If my spouse, Betty Thompson, does not survive me, I give all the rest and residue of my estate to English Pod.

229
00:12:58,480 --> 00:13:09,520
If neither Betty Thompson nor English Pod survives me, I give all the rest and residue of my estate to my heirs as determined by the laws of the state of California relating to descent and distribution.

230
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:15,600
I appoint Robert Porter to act as the executor of this will, to serve without bond.

231
00:13:16,560 --> 00:13:23,180
Should Robert Porter be unable or unwilling to serve, then I appoint Jason Smalls to act as the executor of this will.

232
00:13:24,170 --> 00:13:36,310
I herewith affix my signature to this will on this the 23rd of May, 2010 in the presence of the following witnesses who witnessed and subscribed this will at my request and in my presence.

233
00:13:36,800 --> 00:13:44,840
Alright, we're back.

234
00:13:44,840 --> 00:13:46,520
So this is a very interesting topic.

235
00:13:46,760 --> 00:13:54,120
Now I wonder, within the will or within the estate, do you also acquire a person's debts?

236
00:13:54,120 --> 00:13:55,120
Ah, that's a good question.

237
00:13:55,300 --> 00:13:58,600
Because I know in some countries you do, in some countries you don't.

238
00:13:59,680 --> 00:14:11,800
I know in like some cases, some heirs have found themselves in some legal problems because all of a sudden they inherited that person's debts and stuff like that.

239
00:14:11,800 --> 00:14:15,730
I think that does happen, especially for people whose properties are mortgaged.

240
00:14:16,480 --> 00:14:26,530
For example, if your uncle has a house and he didn't pay for it with cash, he has a mortgage and he dies and you inherit this house, you also inherit the mortgage.

241
00:14:27,560 --> 00:14:31,120
So sometimes inheritances are not always a good thing.

